  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 543 AM MDT Tue Sep 8 2026/ \n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nA shortwave trough will make its way across the region through the \nday continuing the chances for showers along the Rocky Mountain \nFront and across the Hi-Line. There is a chance for some isolated to \nscattered thunderstorms this afternoon, although the threat for \nsevere weather is low.\n\nBy Wednesday, upper-level flow becomes a bit more zonal to \nnorthwesterly, allowing for temperatures to warm up. This effect is \nbriefly enhanced on Thursday as weak transient ridging moves through \nwhich will allow for lower elevation locations to reach the upper \n70s to mid 80s.\n\nThe next system is expected to arrive Thursday evening into Friday \nwith cooler and wetter conditions expected to last through the \nweekend.\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nScattered showers will continue, primarily along the Rocky Mountain \nFront and across the Hi-Line through the rest of the morning with \nisolated to scattered thunderstorms possible this afternoon. These \nstorms are not expected to be severe but they may produce locally \nheavy rain which may continue some of the flooding concerns for \nareas that have already received a lot of rain.\n\nWith regards to the system this weekend, there is a chance for \nmountain snow, primarily above 7000 feet. Confidence in amounts \nabove 4 inches is low but there is currently a 60% chance for at \nleast 2 inches of snow in the mountains Sunday morning. The main \nconcern will be for those recreating in the backcountry where \ndifficult conditions are expected Friday through Sunday. \n-thor",
